pcie4 and pcie6a deliver their MSIs as LPIs through the GIC ITS, so the
GIC must be able to read its LPI tables from DDR whenever an MSI comes
in. The GIC's system NoC port (qnm_gic -> qns_gemnoc_sf, BCM SN0) has
no vote of its own: SN0 is keepalive only in the active and wake sets,
and the only sleep-set voter on hamoa is the usb-ddr path of the DWC3
controllers.

As soon as all three USB controllers are runtime suspended, nothing
keeps SN0 in the sleep set. When the APSS then reaches its deepest
system idle state, RPMh applies the sleep set and turns SN0 off. MSIs
from the NVMe or the Wi-Fi stall, eDP fails PSR entry, ath12k times out
on firmware requests, and the SoC resets within a second. Holding
cpu_dma_latency at 0, keeping a 1 kB/s usb-ddr vote alive, or moving
PCIe to the controller's internal MSI each prevent the reset.

Add a gic-mem path, MASTER_GIC1 -> SLAVE_EBI1 with the ALWAYS tag, and
give it a token 1 kB/s peak vote in every OPP. The OPP core manages it
with the other paths, so the vote follows the controller and is dropped
with the OPP on system suspend, which still lets SN0 and CX collapse in
s2idle.

